[{:seon.task/name "the-unit-tap-reports-every-macro-expanded"
  :seon.task/plan "Shrink brief 3, item 16 (worktree session SHRINK2, 2026-10-23, read on main 8a5818c): seon.program/macro-uses (src/seon/program.clj:282-292) re-reads each form's text and resolves every list head in its namespace to find the macros it calls, because the analyzed node is the expansion and names no macro. That reading can disagree with what the compiler did: a local named like a macro, a quoted list, a head shadowed by a binding (AGENTS §9: no reading of code where the compiler's facts exist). Cause, in Jolt: jolt-core/jolt/analyzer.clj:2333-2342 expands a macro call knowing its var (macro-symbol ctx head) and records it only in *expansion-box* (:120), a diagnostic box holding the innermost expansion, reset as analysis returns; nothing reports it to the unit tap (:121-, jolt.analyzer/*unit-tap*), so the tap's consumer (seon.program/tap) cannot know which macros a unit expanded. Correct, on a jolt/<topic> branch of our Jolt: at that expansion site, when a unit is being tapped, add the macro's qualified symbol to the unit's set of expanded macros (every expansion, nested ones included), and hand that set to the unit tap with the form and node (a third argument, or a key on the node's unit info). Then seon.program/tap records it in the account (:macros), seon.program/dependents reads it, and macro-uses and its re-read go. Proofs: a Jolt test that a unit expanding a macro which expands another reports both; seon.program-test: a form whose local shadows a macro's name records no macro use (red today through macro-uses), and dependents of a changed macro are unchanged otherwise."
  :seon.task/kind :work}]
